  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday through Thursday)\nIssued at 108 PM CDT Fri Oct 23 2026\n\nMid to upper ridging over the MS Valley begins to flatten Sunday.\nThis occurs as energy within southwest flow east of the \ndisturbance over the Desert SW continues to send waves of energy \nproviding lift and focusing of moisture over the SE NM plains, \nextending into the northern Permian Basin. Rain chances remain \nhighest (45% to 60%) for these regions, but increase into the 35% \nto 45% for adjacent foothills and plains south into the Marfa \nPlateau and east into the Permian Basin. Continued strong winds \nincreasing with height providing shear for organization of long- \nlived storms and mid-level temperatures staying cool mean \nstrongest storms Sunday pose a threat for large hail and damaging \nwinds once again. Hail will become the main severe threat as \noutlined in the SPC Day 3 outlook, which has most of the SE NM \nplains in a MRGL risk. Repeated rounds of heavy rain continue to \ncharacterize the forecast, although coverage and timing are still \ntoo uncertain to warrant any flood products at this time. Highs \nmaintain a downward trend, with 60s and 70s F for cooler spots \nnorth of the Pecos River and over the higher elevations, and \nremaining in the 70s to lower 80s F for the lowlands. \n\nFollowing lows dropping into the 50s and 60s F once again Sunday \nnight, Monday features much the same story, but with lower (35% to\n45%) rain chances, this time over much of the area. While the \nupper disturbance will be progressing east/northeast as ridging \nover the MS Valley continues to break down, bulk of best forcing \nwill miss the area, keeping the Permian Basin and points south and\nsoutheast drier. WPC Excessive Rainfall Outlook has highest risk \nof rainfall up to 1\" to 2\" farther north over NE NM and the TX \npanhandle for the weekend through early next week, but this could \nchange. Rain chances stick around Monday night through Tuesday \nbefore diminishing thereafter. Cooler temperatures (widespread 60s\nand 70s F highs, 50s F lows) move in behind the mid to upper \ndisturbance and a weak cold front from Tuesday through the middle \nof next week, keeping conditions seasonable for late October.",
